ee that there's a bias of the reads  distribution where most of the reads are  assigned to these two babies  samples  and the other samples ended up getting  fewer reads  we can see that the bezia  is identified with very high confidence  the number of breeds assigned to babesia  is very high  but  all of these  samples from uh  lyme disease here are negative only two  samples were correctly identified but  they are not part of this table they are  uh  there's a separate table which is  present in the paper  so uh  even with  all the negative samples we were able to  accurately identify two samples  and we were  very successful in um  in uh  verbeta diagnosis with this sample set  so the conclusions from this um are  that we were able to demonstrate the  simultaneous detection and genotyping of  tick bond pathogen and disease agents in  a wide range of samples  and we can see that tbd capsic is  markedly superior to unbiased sequencing  and it exceeded the sensitivity of qpcr  it can be further improved with  modifications to sample preparation and  sequencing protocols  and increasing sequencing depth may also  increase the sensitivity  and it can offer valuable insights into  our understanding of tick-borne diseases  by facilitating analysis of previously  undiagnosed samples  the next part of my talk is uh  about where capsic vertebrate it's a  virum capture sequencing assay similar  to tbd capsic  this was actually published in 2015  and  it's  very well  cited by um in other articles and  i'm only going to scratch the surface in  this partic for this particular uh  section of my talk  given the time  constraint  but for more information you can check  out this article and there are several  other articles that talk about this  essay  so this uh slide here shows the  design process and the validation  process for this um  for wakatseek vertebrate rc so first the  probes were designed and in order to  design the probes we downloaded all the  viral coding sequences from embl  database we designed the probes and from  there we designed a unique probe set  then the probes were validated by  mapping against 100 representative  genomes to see that the probes are  mapping to these genomes  ubiquitously and also they map only to  vertebrate viruses not to plant viruses  or insect viruses or bacteriophages  and after pro in silicon validation  uh we did a probe  probe synthesis where the tm was  adjusted homopolymeric sequences were  removed and were trimmed and the probes  were synthesized on class slide  using a glass slide  method  so  after the pros were synthesized the  probes were tested with simulated  samples to look for specificity  sensitivity and to compare the results  with rt pcr  and also to see the performance in  multiplexing and to see if probes get  saturated or not  and after that the  results were extended to real samples  uh  using it to see how well it performs  when applied in a real realistic  scenario  so the first step  that i mentioned which is the in silico  validation that was performed on 100  genomes  here is an example showing only three  genomes and we can see that  when probes are mapped to the  genomes of poliovirus or yellow fever  virus or parvovirus we see pretty good  coverage which is ubiquitous  uh however we see the difference in the  depth of the  probes because  some  sequences are over represented in the  databases  and they result in higher number of  probes and also  some parts of the genome are more  diverse than the others  and they result in higher number of  probes but overall it's a good  representation because we see that the  genomes are  fully covered in the coding regions  so the first experiment in this uh  particular  uh  validation  was to compare unbiased with bulkapc  vertebrate there were two pools here the  first pool consisted of these three  viruses  and pool two consisted of these four  viruses the first pool used lung  background and the second pool consisted  of blood background  each pool was  run with unbiased and were catholic  sequencing using illumina high feek and  there were two replicate runs for each  experiment  so the results from this experiment are  summarized here we can see uh that  these are the results using unbiased  sequencing and these are the results  with capture sequencing  and we can see that the number of map  reads for each of the viruses is  significantly greater  capture sequencing than without for all  the  agents in this particular experiment  and the fold increase is  pretty high and we are seeing up to 8  000 fold increase in the average  coverage  so uh here we can see uh a schematic  showing the enrichment basically this is  the unbiased sequencing this is where  cathy vertebrate and this is the mapping  of the probe to the genome and here we  can see that uh capture sequencing has  done a pretty good job  of mapping the reads to the  to the reference genome and whereas the  conventional sequencing is pretty sparse  and it's  not that  like they're not doing that well  similarly for this particular uh  enterovirus genome  so  this shows the specificity of recap seek  so  uh here we can see  uh  the pools  where basically  these are the reads before a host  removal and after host removal and when  you compare the conventional method to  uh  recap seek you can see that  uh recap c gives you higher number of  reads after whole subtraction as  compared to the regular method and in  the second pool  it's similar that we get more number of  uh  reads after whole subtraction and also  we can see the percent of host removed  is  much less with uh recursive than with  the  regular unbiased sequencing showing that  recapsic has high specificity  now in terms of the limit of detection  uh  here we can see uh that  there are two experiments in blood and  lung  and we can see the copy numbers ranging  from  like very low of 10 to 5000  and we can see that with copy numbers as  low as 10  we have some dete